      "math_academy_id": "q-76640",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/7ba1daa74999732ba112150ad8ecb0958f80057a4aeb8ff52c96bf9b44a8337c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC.$ If $m∠BOA={131}^{∘}$ and $m∠BOC={73}^{∘},$ find $m∠DOA.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/68c84f70ff4fcd384c21414958ea696469ac6c64749d31c7db7f8792599d77ce.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOC \\\\  & ={131}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={58}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =\\frac{m∠COA}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{58}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={29}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
